WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.481
A control panel records which of its switches are on as the bits of a non-negative integer mask;

2
00:00:04.481 --> 00:00:09.873
bit 0 is the rightmost switch. Two on-switches are neighbours when no other switch between them is on.

3
00:00:09.873 --> 00:00:16.396
Return the largest distance, measured in bit positions, between any pair of neighbouring on-switches in mask. If

4
00:00:16.396 --> 00:00:19.113
fewer than two switches are on, return 0.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.770
41 is 101001 in binary, so switches 0, 3 and 5 are on. The neighbour gaps are 3

6
00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.605
and 2, and the largest is 3.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:37.592
4096 is 1000000000000 in binary: only switch 12 is on, so there is no pair of neighbours.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.163
Only set bits matter, so jump from one set bit to the next instead of looking at the

9
00:00:43.163 --> 00:00:44.317
zeros between them.

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.941
Repeatedly isolate the lowest set bit with remaining &amp; -remaining, find its position with bit.bit_length() - 1,

11
00:00:50.941 --> 00:00:55.101
compare with the previous position, and clear it with remaining &amp;= remaining - 1.

12
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.760
The low-bit trick visits each set bit exactly once. remaining &amp; -remaining isolates it, bit_length() - 1 finds

13
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.576
its zero-based position, and remaining &amp; (remaining - 1) removes it. Comparing each visited position with the

14
00:01:08.576 --> 00:01:15.403
previous one finds the largest neighbouring gap. There are at most 31 iterations in this input domain, with

15
00:01:15.403 --> 00:01:17.451
O(1) extra memory.

16
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:25.662
For 41 the lowest set bit is 1 at position 0, so previous = 0 and mask becomes

17
00:01:25.662 --> 00:01:26.109
40.

18
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:33.407
Next bit 8 is position 3, giving gap 3; then bit 32 is position 5, giving gap 2.

19
00:01:33.407 --> 00:01:34.371
The answer is 3.

20
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.999
O(k) for k set bits, at most 31, which is O(log mask). O(1)
